{
 "answer": "Measured per member rather than book-wide: sh510300 0.928 [0.830, 1.026], sh510050 0.760 [0.674, 0.847], sh510500 0.416 [0.306, 0.527], sh512100 0.414 [0.332, 0.496], sh588000 1.456 [1.310, 1.602], against a pooled slope of 0.622 [0.567, 0.676] with five member intercepts. Compatibility reads mixture: 8 member pairs have disjoint intervals, and 4 of 5 members sit outside the pooled interval (sh510300, sh510500, sh512100, sh588000). Re-priced with each sleeve charged its own exponent the movement bill becomes binary_low linear 1538.8 -> same-exponent 5-sleeve 2828.2 -> per-member 2523.3 bp; tilt_step linear 1610.6 -> same-exponent 5-sleeve 3803.9 -> per-member 3485.0 bp; tilt_linear linear 1706.0 -> same-exponent 5-sleeve 5720.5 -> per-member 6009.8 bp. The split and the mixture are separated: charging the same five-sleeve formula with the pooled exponent accounts for binary_low +1289 bp, tilt_step +2193 bp, tilt_linear +4015 bp of the move and the per-member exponents for binary_low -305 bp, tilt_step -319 bp, tilt_linear +289 bp. So the single exponent is not the book's true bill -- the sleeves do not share an exponent at the book level. The toll still clears its bar on 3 of 3 arms under the per-member charge (linear 3, basket point 3), and 2 of 6 ladder cells change verdict when the charge becomes per-member. On the 2017+ slice the exponent measured then is 0.883 against 0.928 on the full sample (the full-sample interval covers that era point); re-deriving the ladder with the era's own exponent instead of the borrowed one changes 0 of 6 cells.",
